  "narrative": {
    "intro": "California has received $2.1B in federal contract obligations so far in FY2027. The state has 9,728 awards to date, with an average award size of $220K. Obligations are down 96.4% year over year, reflecting that current-year totals are partial and the fiscal year is ongoing. Fiscal-year history shows prior totals of $58.4B in FY22, $63.1B in FY23, $63.2B in FY24, $63.2B in FY25, and $59.1B in FY26, before the FY27 total of $2.1B. The Department of Energy has been the largest buyer so far, obligating $853M, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $502M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $392M. Research and development work dominates industries, led by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences with $842M. Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC is the top vendor with $550M in obligations. These figures describe the state of federal contracting in California through the current fiscal period, with large prior-year baselines and a much smaller fiscal-year-to-date total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the FY2027-to-date total?",
        "body": "California reached $2.1B in obligations so far in FY2027. Obligations are down 96.4% year over year. Historical totals show much larger annual baselines: $58.4B in FY22 and between $59.1B and $63.2B in the intervening years. The current FY27 figure reflects a partial fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors account for most spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy leads buying with $853M, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $502M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $392M. Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC leads vendors with $550M, followed by CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY with $215M and THE REGENTS OF THE UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA with $162M. Research and development is the largest industry area, with Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $842M."
      }
    ]
  },
